WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.740
Rural Argentinian communities slept 35 minutes less per night once electricity arrived, trimming

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:12.920
rest without extra work. This is Planetterrian Daily, Episode 222. A Planetarian spotlight on

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:19.520
evening light follows those findings, then Parkinson's activity and a microbiome agenda. A 12-year

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:24.440
study in nature followed indigenous Argentinian groups through the arrival of mains, power,

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:25.380
and smartphones.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:29.580
Nightly duration changed even though daylight exposure stayed the same.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:33.220
Evening artificial light stood out as the primary driver.

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:38.100
Researchers tracked the same communities across the transition with consistent measurement

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:38.520
methods.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.160
The reduction appeared across age groups.

00:00:42.243 --> 00:00:45.183
Actigraphy, not self-report, recorded the change.

00:00:45.703 --> 00:00:50.923
Because those communities had no prior grid access, the before and after design isolates

00:00:50.923 --> 00:00:55.163
artificial light from other lifestyle changes that often arrive with electrification.

00:00:55.763 --> 00:01:00.223
Over the full follow up, the reduction did not rebound once residents adapted to the

00:01:00.223 --> 00:01:01.023
new technology.

00:01:01.643 --> 00:01:06.683
One open question is whether temperate latitudes, where seasonal daylight variation differs,

00:01:07.283 --> 00:01:08.843
would show a change of that scale.

00:01:09.363 --> 00:01:14.023
Future work could test whether targeted dimming or spectrum shifts blunt the effect without

00:01:14.023 --> 00:01:16.043
removing the benefits of electrification.

00:01:16.043 --> 00:01:23.323
Similar patterns may exist wherever evening illumination runs later into the night without a matching shift in sleep timing.

00:01:24.137 --> 00:01:29.777
Most people assume rural electrification mainly adds convenience and better light for evening tasks.

00:01:30.397 --> 00:01:36.877
Artificial light after sunset suppresses melatonin and delays the circadian signal that tells the brain to start sleep.

00:01:37.557 --> 00:01:41.817
Risked actigraphy across multiple households caught that shift as the grid arrived.

00:01:41.817 --> 00:01:46.937
The compression appeared rapidly, showing that even modest evening illumination can shrink

00:01:46.937 --> 00:01:48.917
the sleep window people actually use.

00:01:49.577 --> 00:01:54.037
Limiting bright overhead lights and screens in the two hours before bed is the practical

00:01:54.037 --> 00:01:56.037
starting point for protecting that window.

00:01:56.697 --> 00:02:01.517
The evening period is the critical stretch, where artificial sources override the natural

00:02:01.517 --> 00:02:04.277
decline in light that once triggered melatonin release.

00:02:04.917 --> 00:02:09.717
The same physiology operates in any household where bright indoor lighting or device screens

00:02:09.717 --> 00:02:11.117
stay on past sunset.

00:02:11.797 --> 00:02:13.757
Dimming or switching off overhead lights

00:02:13.757 --> 00:02:16.057
and lowering screen brightness after dusk

00:02:16.057 --> 00:02:19.637
lets the bodies endogenous melatonin rhythm line up more closely

00:02:19.637 --> 00:02:20.797
with external darkness.

00:02:21.577 --> 00:02:24.217
Experiments that very light spectrum or intensity

00:02:24.217 --> 00:02:25.697
in newly connected settings

00:02:25.697 --> 00:02:27.917
would show whether modest changes can restore

00:02:27.917 --> 00:02:29.117
part of the lost sleep

00:02:29.477 --> 00:02:32.797
without sacrificing the practical advantages of electrification.

00:02:33.737 --> 00:02:37.097
People with Parkinson's who exercise more than 8 hours a week

00:02:37.097 --> 00:02:39.577
showed almost no average drop in memory,

00:02:39.577 --> 00:02:44.237
theory, thinking, or movement scores over seven years of tracking, according to a report

00:02:44.237 --> 00:02:45.277
from science daily.

00:02:45.897 --> 00:02:48.857
A lower activity group declined steadily on the same measures.

00:02:49.457 --> 00:02:53.817
The dose response relationship held after statistical adjustment for disease severity

00:02:53.817 --> 00:02:54.417
at baseline.

00:02:55.117 --> 00:03:00.017
Higher activity tracked with slower decline across those motor and cognitive domains together.

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:06.120
The U.S. Department of Health and Human Services convened microbiome scientists who set out

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:08.420
10 research priorities in nature medicine.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:14.060
An international group of leading researchers shaped a list focused on causation, therapeutic

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:16.580
modulation, and public health translation.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:22.680
The priorities emphasize longitudinal human cohorts, mechanistic studies in model systems,

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:24.960
and standardized measurement protocols.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:28.560
The document is intended to guide funding and collaboration.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:33.860
It targets gaps in how microbial communities influence conditions ranging from metabolic

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:36.540
disorders to immune mediated diseases.

00:03:37.541 --> 00:03:43.161
Human cells pack roughly 2 meters of DNA inside the nucleus, bearing some identity genes

00:03:43.161 --> 00:03:45.761
beyond easy reach of gene activation tools.

00:03:46.221 --> 00:03:51.841
A short compression step reported via Fizz.org, loosens that packing enough for clustered

00:03:51.841 --> 00:03:58.501
regularly interspace short palindromic repeats activation, or CRISPRA, guide RNAs to reach

00:03:58.501 --> 00:04:00.481
previously silent neuronal genes.

00:04:01.141 --> 00:04:05.381
Conversion efficiency rose when skin cells were turned toward a neuronal identity.

00:04:05.381 --> 00:04:08.641
The CRISPR components themselves were left unchanged.

00:04:09.141 --> 00:04:13.581
The packing is essential for life, yet it creates a problem for tools that struggle to reach

00:04:13.581 --> 00:04:16.061
deeply buried genes controlling cell identity.

00:04:16.641 --> 00:04:21.501
The finding points to a simple physical adjunct that could be added to existing reprogramming

00:04:21.501 --> 00:04:22.081
protocols.

00:04:23.006 --> 00:04:28.846
Project CD researchers in Dominica attached biologers to resident sperm whales and recorded

00:04:28.846 --> 00:04:35.386
coated vocalizations alongside dive profiles. Sequences of 8-10 clicks reliably proceeded deep

00:04:35.386 --> 00:04:42.486
foraging dives lasting 45-60 minutes. Each day, a sperm whale needs to eat 3-4% of its body weight,

00:04:42.926 --> 00:04:48.226
and animals weighing up to 57 tons therefore take more than a ton of food in 24 hours.

00:04:48.846 --> 00:04:54.566
The whales descend from warm, sunlit waters into near-complete darkness at depths around 700 meters,

00:04:54.566 --> 00:04:59.786
and the same quotas were rare during surface breathing intervals of 15 to 20 minutes.

00:05:00.426 --> 00:05:05.526
Giovanni Petri, a professor in the Network Science Institute at Northeastern University London,

00:05:06.146 --> 00:05:11.226
explained that most socialization happens on the surface, where animals drift toward each other

00:05:11.226 --> 00:05:17.066
and produce quotas rapidly once within one body length. The pattern supplies a measurable acoustic

00:05:17.066 --> 00:05:22.826
marker that future models can use to forecast when whales will descend, with boats, drones carrying

00:05:22.826 --> 00:05:28.326
tags, hydrophones, and semi-autonomous marine gliders streaming the recordings into a central

00:05:28.326 --> 00:05:29.366
vault for analysis.

00:05:30.432 --> 00:05:35.272
Senior dogs that fail to locate hidden treats show measurable memory and spatial deficits

00:05:35.272 --> 00:05:37.832
before owners notice disorientation at home.

00:05:38.532 --> 00:05:42.192
The task uses everyday objects and requires no specialized equipment.

00:05:42.892 --> 00:05:48.112
Earlier detection could let owners and veterinarians adjust routines while the animal still navigates

00:05:48.112 --> 00:05:49.032
familiar spaces.

00:05:49.032 --> 00:05:53.812
diseases. By the time signs such as getting lost indoors or increased anxiety appear,

00:05:54.432 --> 00:05:59.052
the decline may have been underway for years. The hide-and-seek test opens a window on

00:05:59.052 --> 00:06:04.996
to cognitive changes that ordinary observation misses. A large owner survey compared body

00:06:04.996 --> 00:06:09.476
condition scores and reported health issues across thousands of cats on vegan versus

00:06:09.476 --> 00:06:14.996
conventional diets. Vegan-fed animals had measurably lower obesity prevalence and fewer

00:06:14.996 --> 00:06:21.376
associated veterinary complaints. The observational design leaves causation open. The size of the

00:06:21.376 --> 00:06:26.476
difference warrants controlled follow-up studies. The survey focused on rates of overweight and

00:06:26.476 --> 00:06:32.663
related health problems rather than lifespan or other endpoints. Crocus sativist flowers tolerate

00:06:32.663 --> 00:06:38.303
dry conditions and fetch premium prices, and pilot plantings in Colorado are testing the crop

00:06:38.303 --> 00:06:44.583
as extra income on land where traditional irrigation is increasingly limited. Historical uses for wound

00:06:44.583 --> 00:06:49.783
healing and fragrance sit in the background only. The current work centers on agronomic

00:06:49.783 --> 00:06:54.763
viability in the American West, where drought has narrowed options for conventional crops.

00:06:55.483 --> 00:06:59.243
Alexander the Great reportedly bathed in saffron to heal battle wounds,

00:06:59.883 --> 00:07:02.523
and doctors once prescribed it for the bubonic plague.

00:07:03.123 --> 00:07:07.823
The new application centers on economic resilience for farmers facing drier seasons.

00:07:07.823 --> 00:07:14.879
Sohei, Sukunaga, and colleagues at Tahoku University examined 13 silicate glass compositions

00:07:14.879 --> 00:07:18.019
to see what actually sets how well the material conducts heat.

00:07:18.799 --> 00:07:23.259
Their paper in applied physics letters reports that the speed of sound through the glass plays

00:07:23.259 --> 00:07:25.399
the dominant role in thermal conductivity.

00:07:26.039 --> 00:07:31.459
At room temperature, the team measured thermal conductivity, sound velocity, volumetric

00:07:31.459 --> 00:07:34.259
specific heat, and phonon mean free path.

00:07:34.999 --> 00:07:40.039
Volumetric specific heat is how much energy a given volume of glass can store as temperature

00:07:40.039 --> 00:07:45.959
rises, and phonon mean free path is the average distance of phonon travels before it scatters.

00:07:46.379 --> 00:07:51.759
Those two quantities traded off, as one increased, the other tended to decrease, and their

00:07:51.759 --> 00:07:56.519
products stayed approximately constant across the compositions, while thermal conductivity

00:07:56.519 --> 00:07:58.659
stayed proportional to sound velocity.

00:07:58.659 --> 00:08:04.839
Sukinaga, an associate professor at Tahoku, noted that thermal conductivity has been studied

00:08:04.839 --> 00:08:10.159
for a long time, yet remains one of the least-understood properties of non-crystalline materials,

00:08:10.699 --> 00:08:15.599
and the result offers a guideline for designing glass heat flow by controlling sound velocity,

00:08:16.039 --> 00:08:19.599
rather than treating several thermal parameters as independent variables.

00:08:20.259 --> 00:08:24.459
The next step is to determine whether this relationship also applies to other types

00:08:24.459 --> 00:08:28.279
of materials, and whether it holds at temperatures beyond room temperature.

00:08:29.214 --> 00:08:34.794
Engineers at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, or MIT, built a paper-thin swimming

00:08:34.794 --> 00:08:38.274
robot powered by living muscle cells, according to Science Daily.

00:08:38.914 --> 00:08:43.774
The cells contract in response to light flashes, producing propulsion and steering through

00:08:43.774 --> 00:08:44.134
water.

00:08:44.694 --> 00:08:49.454
The thin profile allows navigation in confined or delicate environments where rigid machines

00:08:49.454 --> 00:08:50.494
would cause damage.

00:08:50.494 --> 00:08:56.554
The robot responds directly to optical signals without external wiring or mechanical actuators.

00:08:56.954 --> 00:09:02.854
The design explores fragile settings that conventional devices cannot safely enter, and it demonstrates

00:09:02.854 --> 00:09:08.114
a route toward self-healing biological machines for future biomedical or environmental sensing.

00:09:09.248 --> 00:09:14.908
Bacteria corrode dental implant surfaces, shedding microscopic titanium particles into surrounding

00:09:14.908 --> 00:09:15.348
tissue.

00:09:15.348 --> 00:09:21.108
Those particles activate immune cells and sustain inflammation that speeds jawbone resorption.

00:09:21.588 --> 00:09:26.228
The mechanism helps explain why some implant infections resist standard antibiotics.

00:09:26.988 --> 00:09:30.688
It also points to surface modifications that might reduce particle release.

00:09:31.368 --> 00:09:36.888
The corrosion can occur even without overt clinical signs in some cases, a process uncovered

00:09:36.888 --> 00:09:41.048
while studying why dental implant infections often persist despite treatment.

00:09:42.126 --> 00:09:47.806
Lipodystrophy mice given one dose of a leptin expressing viral vector reversed hepatic stiotosis

00:09:47.806 --> 00:09:54.566
and restored insulin sensitivity according to bioengineer.org. The effect persisted for the duration

00:09:54.566 --> 00:10:00.106
of the study without repeated dosing. Sustained leptin replacement via gene transfer corrected

00:10:00.106 --> 00:10:05.946
metabolic defects caused by the absence of adipose tissue. The animals lacked normal fat tissue

00:10:05.946 --> 00:10:11.266
and its associated hormone production. That single injection produced measurable improvements in

00:10:11.266 --> 00:10:17.384
both liver-fat accumulation and glucose handling. In brief, public health researchers documented

00:10:17.384 --> 00:10:23.164
lasting links between restricted voting access, reduced life expectancy, and poorer chronic

00:10:23.164 --> 00:10:29.364
disease outcomes in affected communities. seismic surveys beneath Japan's Kikai Caldera reveal a

00:10:29.364 --> 00:10:36.024
growing magma reservoir, 7,300 years after its last major eruption, offering new data

00:10:36.024 --> 00:10:38.204
on how supervolcanoes rebuild pressure.

00:10:38.844 --> 00:10:43.564
Before we go, the Tahoku measurements will test whether sound velocity still governs

00:10:43.564 --> 00:10:46.904
heat flow and other materials and at temperatures above room temperature.

